description:
Currently, if users want to construct a custom run hook, they need to create an R6 class, e.g.

```R
CustomSessionRunHook <- R6::R6Class(
  "CustomSessionRunHook",
  inherit = EstimatorSessionRunHook,
  public = list(
    end = function(session) {
      cat("Running custom session run hook at the end of a session")
    })
)
custom_hook <- CustomSessionRunHook$new()
```

I think we might want to instead provide a function that constructs a custom session run hook class, e.g.

```R
hook <- session_run_hook(
    end = function(session) { ... }
)
```

and take care of creating the R6 object behind the scenes. FWIW, I think R6 classes are often used by package developers for managing state behind the scenes, but are less typically used + created directly by users.

@terrytangyuan I noticed that much of the function reference we've ported over from Python still retains a Python orientation (i.e. mentioning Python class names explicitly, using Python list literals, referring to dictionaries, etc.). We need to ensure a couple of things:

1) We have reference docs for all of our exported functions; and

2) It's been converted to use R nomenclature, constructs, etc.

Per @jjallaire, the extraction of predicted classes and probabilities seems a bit fiddly / complicated -- is there anything that can be done to make this simpler?

Currently, users need to do the following to extract them:
```
# extract predicted classes
predicted_classes <- unlist(lapply(predictions, function(prediction) {
  prediction$class
}))

# extract predicted probabilities
predicted_probs <- lapply(predictions, function(prediction) {
  prediction$prob
})
```
